<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mutsu Munemitsu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1844">1844</a>–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mutsu Munemitsu was a Japanese statesman from the Kii domain who served as foreign minister from 1892 to 1896. He negotiated the Anglo-Japanese Treaty of Commerce and Navigation of 1894, which ended British extraterritoriality in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a> concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a>. His memoir Kenkenroku recounts the diplomacy of that war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mutsu-munemitsu.jpg" alt="Mutsu Munemitsu" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mutsu_Munemitsu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mutsu was born on 20 August 1844 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wakayama">Wakayama</a> domain in Kii Province, the sixth son of Date Munehiro, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> retainer of the Kii Tokugawa Clan who was active in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonno-joi">Sonnō jōi</a> movement. Mutsu joined Sakamoto Ryōma and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a> in the movement to overthrow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> he governed Hyōgo and then Kanagawa prefectures, both of which held foreign settlements, headed the Land Tax Reform of 1873–1881 and sat on the Genrōin. He was imprisoned from 1878 until 1883 for conspiring to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a> in the Satsuma Rebellion, and in prison he translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremy-bentham">Jeremy Bentham</a>'s Utilitarianism into Japanese. Rejoining the government in the Foreign Ministry, he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for studies in 1884 and was Minister to Washington D.C. from 1888 to 1890, when he established diplomatic relations between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and partially revised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unequal-treaties">unequal treaties</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1890 he became Minister of Agriculture and Commerce and won a seat in the House of Representatives for the 1st Wakayama District. As Foreign Minister in the cabinet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a> from 1892, he was the lead Japanese negotiator of the Treaty of Shimonoseki. After a Japanese fanatic attempted to kill the Chinese negotiator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a>, the government reduced the indemnity it meant to claim, and Mutsu remarked that Li's misfortune was the good fortune of the Great Ch'ing Empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-intervention">Triple Intervention</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> reversed the gains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki">Shimonoseki</a>, the Japanese public blamed Mutsu. He resigned all government posts in May 1896 and moved to Ōiso, Kanagawa, where he wrote his diplomatic memoir Kenkenroku; because of the secrets it contained, it could not be published until 1923. He had been made a count at the end of the war and received the Grand Cordon of the Order of the Rising Sun in 1895. He died of tuberculosis in Takinogawa, Tokyo Prefecture, on 24 August 1897.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mutsu Munemitsu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After abolition in 1871 it became Wakayama Prefecture; the diplomat <b>Mutsu Munemitsu</b> came from Kii.</blockquote><a href="/state/kii-domain">Kii Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kishū Domain. Died in Kita, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.75,139.73&amp;year=1878" aria-label="Kita on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8077 872 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Mutsu Munemitsu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8527 1142h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8527 1142h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=35.75,139.73&amp;year=1878">What was here in 1878? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
